# Part Three — The Examiner's Record of Topic 7

## Every question, 2016–2026

@py topic_questions 7

## What the record reveals

- **Nine full questions and three short notes** (==10.5 visits==), and **12 of the 140 MCQs** — one of the heaviest topics, after Topics 4 and 1.
- **Forms and strategies are asked again and again:** ==2016== (sites and forms; how to eliminate), ==2017== (forms; workable strategies) and ==2023== (define; forms; practical strategies) — three near repeats. ==2024== added the **Punjab Act 2016** and **consequences**.
- **Theory is rising:** **power and control** (==2020==), **root causes and how they intersect** (==2025==) and **masculinity studies** (==2026== note).
- **Each case study has been asked once:** Mukhtaran Mai with structural and direct violence (==2019==), Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy's two documentaries (==2020==), and Malala's achievements (==2025==).
- **Short notes:** honour killing (==2017==), government initiatives (==2018==), masculinities (==2026==).
- **Never yet asked in full:** acid attacks; workplace harassment; cyber violence; the cycle of violence; the ecological model; a comparison of the three case studies.

## Five likely questions

1. "Critically evaluate the effectiveness of laws against gender-based violence in Pakistan. Why do conviction rates remain so low?" *(Chapters 4 and 7.)*
2. "Explain the ecological model of violence against women and apply it to Pakistan." *(Chapters 2 and 4.)*
3. "What do the lives of Mukhtaran Mai, Malala Yousafzai and Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy teach us about resisting gender-based violence in Pakistan?" *(Chapters 10–12.)*
4. "Discuss workplace sexual harassment in Pakistan and evaluate the Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act 2010." *(Chapters 5 and 7.)*
5. "Cyber violence is the new frontier of gender-based violence. Discuss with reference to Pakistan." *(Chapter 5.)*

These are not solved here; the Prediction Papers include answers to predicted questions. Plan each as an answer map for practice.
